WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.818
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Cost optimization

00:00:01.818 --> 00:00:03.210
is about more than saving money.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.480
It's about maximizing the
return on what you're spending.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.850
Let's walk through how to
optimize your costs for Google

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.032
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE.

00:00:11.032 --> 00:00:12.990
Today, we'll be talking
about some general tips

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.930
for getting started with cost
optimization and some best

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.110
practices.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.650
In other words, let's
go beyond your GKE bill.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.136
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.120
Individuals and companies
alike are talking about,

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.480
experimenting with, and
deploying apps on Kubernetes.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.820
And of course, GKE
is made to scale

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.860
as you add more traffic
and more applications.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.630
However, scaling your apps might
also mean scaling your costs.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.570
And as more teams in your
organization use Kubernetes,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.930
it may become more
complex to manage.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.680
Let's look at some
ways to think about how

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.910
you can optimize your usage,
and your costs, for GKE.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.220
I'll get started by quickly
going over some basics,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.080
and then talk about the
business and culture side.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.460
Then, I'll go over some
general technical tips.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.739
Simply put, what you're
paying for with GKE

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:07.040
itself is primarily two things--

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.540
the managed architecture for
actually running the Kubernetes

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.690
cluster, and the resources
that your apps are running on,

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.180
such as CPUs,
memory, and storage.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.680
There's also a few
other things that

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.230
will affect your costs,
like networking and resource

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.340
location.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.040
We'll dive into details to
better understand and optimize

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.620
different areas in these videos.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.098
But no matter what
the costs are,

00:01:27.098 --> 00:01:28.890
it's hard to make
decisions without knowing

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.510
where they're coming from.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.360
Being able to have visibility
into all the different moving

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.510
pieces is incredibly important.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.200
And a great place
to start might be

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.050
using a dashboard to help
visualize your resources.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.660
The next videos will
go over some tips

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.410
for exactly how to do that.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.280
But try to always remember
that the more you understand

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.770
about the full details
of your applications,

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.410
the more opportunities
you'll have to optimize.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.250
And don't worry.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.830
You'll hear me say
that plenty of times.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.413
On top of that, you
shouldn't forget

00:01:57.413 --> 00:01:58.830
about the people
that are actually

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.690
writing the applications.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.330
When you're in a rush to deploy
an application, sometimes,

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:04.890
shortcuts get taken.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.650
Or maybe your organization
has grown so large

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.930
that they completely abstract
Kubernetes away from the teams,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.370
and then they're not even sure
how their apps make it to GKE.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.580
Either way, keeping developers,
operators, and everyone else

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.820
in the dark about how their
applications actually run

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.070
might cause more harm than good.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.960
Instead, consider
spending the time

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.910
to train your technical
teams on Kubernetes

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.600
best practices, like
online classes, code labs,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.995
even videos like this one.

00:02:31.995 --> 00:02:34.350
When teams understand
and care about the cost

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.810
it takes to run their
apps, the more those teams

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.580
will be able to optimize.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.890
Beyond that, you can
also implement guardrails

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.740
to protect yourself from
updates that might significantly

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.270
affect your costs.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.330
There are ways to automatically
enforce policies and set up

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.370
quotas for resources
that we'll also

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.800
cover in this video series.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.230
Building a culture of
awareness and accountability

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.530
is critical to being able
to optimize your costs.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.930
Keeping all of that in
mind, let's switch over

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.420
to some best practices
on the technical side.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.460
You can find reference
links in the description

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.150
if you want to read
more about any of these.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.500
So if you're already
using GKE, you're

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.750
probably aware of your
production clusters.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.480
But how many development
clusters do you have?

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.660
Smaller teams might work well
with one cluster per developer.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.420
But you should actually look
at multi-tenant clusters

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.160
to save on overhead.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.520
By using namespaces and policies
to keep resources capped

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.970
and isolated, dev teams can
have the room to experiment

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:33.260
that won't lead to
any surprise bills.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.120
On top of that,
there's a few add-ons

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.240
that come with GKE clusters that
are important for production

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.030
environments, but might not
be needed for your development

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.140
environments.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.880
For each of your
smaller clusters,

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.920
consider disabling
or limiting add-ons

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.320
if you're not using them,
such as Cloud Logging

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.920
and Monitoring, Horizontal Pod
Autoscaling, the Kubernetes

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.290
Dashboard, and Kube DNS.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:57.650
Most can be disabled
with a one-line command.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.750
These small add-ons can add up.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.600
So the better you understand
what your apps need

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.770
and the environment around
them, the more you can optimize.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:08.750
Speaking of what your
apps need, do you

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:10.850
know what your pod
disruption budgets are?

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.000
You can set the number of pods
or the percentage of pods that

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.670
can be taken down when
doing voluntary disruptions,

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.010
like upgrades or autoscaling.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.110
Think about the
minimum amount of pots

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.910
that you need to run without
disrupting your applications'

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:24.810
users.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.250
And think about that for each
application independently.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.290
Pod disruption budgets
can help keep your apps

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.270
running as you
configure autoscaling

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:35.110
without overprovisioning.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:36.810
We'll talk more about
autoscaling soon.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.560
If you're not sure
about how to figure out

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.830
things like resource utilization
or pod disruption budgets,

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.000
stay tuned.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.470
On the next video,
we'll talk all

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.690
about monitoring
your GKE environment

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.698
and getting better
observability.

00:04:48.698 --> 00:04:50.740
Remember to check out the
link in the description

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.960
for the full guide on
GKE cost optimization.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:56.310
[MUSIC PLAYING]

